    {
      "id": "cmtjcmjcc000a04k4pftq7hsd",
      "term": "What are the main sources of value creation in an LBO (5)?",
      "setTitle": "LBO",
      "klps": [
        {
          "text": "Value creation in an LBO means the sources of the gain between entry price and exit proceeds",
          "kind": "definition"
        },
        {
          "text": "EBITDA growth is a driver: growing revenue and improving margins enlarges the core earnings base",
          "kind": "mechanism"
        },
        {
          "text": "Since exit equity is driven off exit EBITDA, a bigger EBITDA directly increases the sale value",
          "kind": "causal"
        },
        {
          "text": "Debt paydown is a driver: the company's free cash flow is used to repay principal over the hold",
          "kind": "mechanism"
        },
        {
          "text": "Debt paydown converts debt into sponsor equity so the sponsor's share of the business grows even if the total value is flat",
          "kind": "causal"
        },
        {
          "text": "Multiple expansion is a driver: selling at a higher EBITDA multiple than paid creates value on its own",
          "kind": "mechanism"
        },
        {
          "text": "Expense reductions are a driver: cutting overhead, renegotiating supplier contracts, and streamlining the cost structure improve margins and flow straight into EBITDA",
          "kind": "mechanism"
        },
        {
          "text": "Operational improvements and add-on acquisitions are a driver: better management, systems, and processes plus bolt-on deals build scale and synergies beyond organic growth",
          "kind": "mechanism"
        },
        {
          "text": "In practice, expense reductions and operational improvements are largely how EBITDA growth is achieved",
          "kind": "causal"
        }
      ],
